<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Knights Templar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1119–<a href="/years/1312">1312</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Templars, Order of the Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Knights Templar were a Catholic military order founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> around 1119 to protect pilgrims in the crusader states. They became a wealthy international organisation with castles, estates and banking operations across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. In 1191 they bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> but returned it after a revolt in 1192. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a> attacked the order, and the pope dissolved it in 1312.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/knights-templar.jpg" alt="Knights Templar" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rkcora · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandeira_Templ%C3%A1ria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> in 1099, many Christians made pilgrimages to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, but the roads beyond Jerusalem were not safe. Bandits preyed on pilgrims travelling from the coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> to the interior, and some were killed by the hundreds. In 1119 the French knight Hugues de Payens approached King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin II of Jerusalem</a> and Warmund, Patriarch of Jerusalem, and proposed a monastic order to protect them.</p><p>The king and patriarch agreed, probably at the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a> in January 1120, and Baldwin gave the order a headquarters in a wing of the royal palace on the Temple Mount, in the captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aqsa-mosque">Al-Aqsa Mosque</a>. The first group numbered about nine knights and had few resources, relying on donations. Its emblem showed two knights on a single horse, a sign of poverty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The order's work soon grew from escorting pilgrims to regular campaigning. Its first castle, Bagras in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-mountains">Amanus Mountains</a>, lay on the northern frontier of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, and from 1143 the Templars also took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. In the mid-12th century the tide turned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> united much of the Islamic world, the Christian factions quarrelled, and the Templars were sometimes at odds with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>. Jerusalem fell to Saladin in 1187, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a>.</p><p>The order moved its headquarters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, which was lost in 1291. Its last foothold, the island of Arwad, fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> in 1302 or 1303. In 1307 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>, who owed the order large debts according to some historians, had Jacques de Molay and many other French Templars arrested on Friday 13 October. Pope Clement V, under pressure from Philip, disbanded the order in 1312.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Templars were organised like a monastic order, on the model of Bernard's Cistercian Order. A grand master, appointed for life, directed the military effort in the East and the financial holdings in the West, and each province had its own master. There were three ranks: noble knights in white mantles, non-noble sergeants in black or brown, and chaplains.</p><p>The Latin Rule, devised by Bernard de Clairvaux and Hugues de Payens, set out 72 clauses on dress, horses and conduct. After 1312 some knights and property passed to the Order of Christ, founded in Portugal in 1319 under King Denis, and to the Order of Montesa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Knights Templar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>To hold the frontier, kings relied on the military orders, especially the <b>Templars</b> and Hospitallers, and on town militias raised under the fueros, and Alfonso II founded Teruel around 1170 to guard the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Second Crusade the <b>Knights Templar</b> and the Knights Hospitaller gained property in Croatia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-croatia-1102-1526">Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1120s and 1130s the military orders, the <b>Knights Templar</b> and the Knights Hospitaller, and later the Teutonic Knights, provided permanent garrisons and the money to maintain them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The early kingdom was made by war against al-Andalus, fought by royal hosts, town militias and the military orders of the <b>Templars</b>, Hospitallers, Santiago and Avis, and later the Order of Christ, which Denis founded in 1319 to take over the Templars’ property.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A Latin hierarchy headed by the Archbishop of Patras replaced the Orthodox bishops, and Cistercian, Franciscan and Dominican houses, along with estates of the <b>Templars</b>, Hospitallers and Teutonic Knights, were founded, but Orthodox priests kept their parishes and most of the population remained Orthodox.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Active organisations include the World Woman's Christian Temperance Union, Mothers Against Drunk Driving, Alcohol Justice, International Blue Cross, the Independent Order of Rechabites and the International Organisation of Good <b>Templars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/temperance-movement">Temperance movement · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
